{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Halve employee National Insurance; legal annual cap on migration and monthly Rwanda flights; NHS funding up each year with 92,000 more nurses and 28,000 more doctors plus a Dental Recovery Plan; 1.6 million homes with Section 21 abolished after court reforms; maintain the energy price cap and provide household efficiency/solar vouchers. TEXT_E: A right to see a GP within seven days (24 hours if urgent) with 8,000 more GPs and guaranteed urgent NHS dentistry; fix social care with free personal care and a Carer’s Minimum Wage; build 380,000 homes a year including 150,000 social homes and ban no‑fault evictions; end the sewage scandal with tougher regulation and binding targets; decouple electricity from gas prices and deliver a 10‑year home energy upgrade.",
  "reasoning": "Voters’ top concerns in 2024 were the NHS, cost of living/energy bills, housing and visibly cleaner rivers/seas, all of which are addressed more directly by B’s concrete access guarantees for GP and dentistry, large social care and housing plans, and strong action on sewage and energy bills. A focuses more on tax cuts and migration controls alongside NHS investment, but provides fewer specific service access guarantees and weaker commitments on housing volume and sewage that were salient to voters.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "B"
}
